From 2024ff523fb6084459065501ca785c846215c887 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ibf-coder <ibf-coder@local>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 22:24:07 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] fix(kanban): the archive refusal says what it read, the whole
 batch is asked, and --force is recorded
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Tails of the two kanban windows, each re-verified against the live code before the edit
(red test first, live probe where the finding came from a probe).

The refusal evidence (kanban_db.py, kanban.py):
* the refusal payload gains `host_local` and the line names the signal, the pid and the
  heartbeat age, so a live FOREIGN process or a reused pid is distinguishable from this
  card's worker ("worker_pid 21880 is alive on this host but the claim belongs to another
  host ... if it is not this card's worker, pass --force");
* the host-local gate no longer swallows the guard: `_pid_alive` counts wherever the claim
  came from (probe B archived a card with rc=0 while its pid was alive and the claim had
  expired), while the heartbeat signal guards only HOST-LOCAL claims, which is the parity
  with the condition `release_stale_claims` actually spares. The docstring no longer claims
  a superset it did not have — it names the delta in both directions.
* a batch is one decision: every id is asked about BEFORE the first card is written, so a
  live card refuses the whole batch instead of archiving its siblings (the 2026-09-16
  cleanup archived the first 126 of 128 and killed the two running ones it never asked
  about, returning 0). The refusal events are still written by the DB layer
  (`record_archive_refusals`), so card-by-card and batch leave the same audit trail.

The record (kanban_db.py):
* `--force` is part of the `archived` payload and of the initiator block, so an archive that
  terminated a live worker is not read as an archive of a finished card;
* the `archive_refused`-then-`archived` pair is documented as the form it is: two true
  records of two calls, `write_txn` committing the refusal before the forced retry.

The flag (kanban.py, kanban_parser.py, docs):
* `archive --rm <ids> --force` no longer swallows the flag: the purge branch reports it as
  inert (a purge requires an already-archived card and has no live-run guard to override)
  and the parser help says so at both verbs;
* `website/docs/user-guide/features/kanban.md`: the `archive_refused` event is documented,
  the batch-asks-first rule and the `--force`/`--rm` semantics are stated, and the
  circuit-breaker section notes that a long card's failure budget is stated at creation
  (`max_retries`, `max_runtime_seconds`) because the count cannot tell a long healthy run
  from a short one.

Tests: tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_archive_live_run_guard.py, 19 cells (13 new), red
first; live probe (real `hermes kanban archive`, real live pid, real DB) red on the base
(rc=0, archived with the process alive) and green after the fix (rc=1, refusal with the
evidence; the dead process then archives; a mixed batch archives nothing).
